WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:09.480
It's a busy news day in Hong Kong – and
Lam Yin Pong is out covering the hot stories.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:16.830
Media in Hong Kong are busy following President
Xi Jin Ping who has come to Hong Kong and

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:23.710
pan democrats are preparing for the rally
and some kind of demonstrations.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:30.300
Such events may be common in many countries,
but in Hong Kong they are highly sensitive.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.360
Media coverage of them will be under close
scrutiny.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.600
Many journalists here in Hong Kong say they're
less and less free to report, especially when

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.190
it comes to issues like the influence of the
Beijing government, or the calls for free

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.990
elections here in Hong Kong, or even for Hong
Kong’s independence.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.310
Lam Yin Pong has been a reporter in Hong Kong
for more than 10 years.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:58.780
Today, he works for Radio Free Asia, a U.S.
public media outlet.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:05.119
Previously, he had a job at a big commercial
TV channel in Hong Kong, until, he says, his

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:10.950
editors watered down a report on police violence
against a protester during the pro-democracy

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.249
umbrella movement in 2014.

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:17.079
He and some of his co-workers filed a complaint.

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:18.990
That’s when the trouble began.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:27.139
Those reporters who signed the petition, including
me, we feel that the company was trying to

00:01:27.139 --> 00:01:30.359
force us out after the incident.

00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:38.659
They will just maybe put you into some kind
of meaningless or not significant assignment

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:45.440
and make you feel frustrated and send a clear
message to you that you have no future in

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.049
this company so you better leave.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:53.479
It’s difficult to exactly pinpoint Beijing’s
influence on the Hong Kong media.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.020
It’s not censorship in the classic sense.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:01.219
Many journalists here say the methods are
more subtle.

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:05.930
I meet Ching Cheung, a veteran Hong Kong journalist.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.509
He spent three years in a Chinese prison for
espionage.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.110
A fabricated allegation, he says.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.379
Ching Cheung believes he was locked up because
he wrote a critical article about China’s

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:19.500
handling of a border dispute with Russia.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.319
He says Beijing is trying to pressure critical
journalists in Hong Kong.

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:31.240
We have Beijing’s watchdog organization
here, the central liaison office.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:41.189
The central liaison office will try to have
regular informal dinner or lunch gatherings

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:49.480
with the top management of the media outlets
in Hong Kong, advising them, what to report

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.129
and what not to report.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:54.879
And people will then begin to second guess.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:03:01.870
And once you second guess, you try to always
be on the safe side and that means that you

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.549
voluntarily exercise some sort of self-censorship.

00:03:06.549 --> 00:03:09.939
But the pressure does not only come from the
authorities.

00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:14.609
In recent years, several of Hong Kong’s
media outlets have been bought by businessmen

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:18.920
with close ties to China’s ruling communist
party.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.459
The most prominent example is probably this
one – the South China Morning Post.

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:28.120
It’s the most prestigious English-language
daily in Hong Kong, and it’s been around

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.190
for over 100 years.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:34.560
Last year it was bought by Alibaba – China’s
equivalent to Amazon.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.209
Alibaba is led by Jack Ma, China’s richest
man.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.390
At the Hong Kong Journalist’s Association,
they’re afraid that the South China Morning

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:48.860
Post will be forced to give up the critical
reporting it was once known for.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:57.670
Jack Ma’s people have already made it quite
clear that they want the SCMP to become a

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:05.310
kind of voice of China, a window of China,
to tell the China story I guess from a mainland

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:14.920
perspective in order to balance what they
see as biased coverage in western media on

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.690
China and Hong Kong.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:22.500
For Hong Kong’s journalists, Beijing’s
rising influence means critical reporting

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.220
in Hong Kong is more and more becoming a phenomenon
of the past.

